d operation. GNNs can also be combined with RNNs for processing time-varying graph signals [46], [47]. Nevertheless, data-driven methods, including GNNbased ones, often suffer from difficulties in training, high computational cost, and generalization issues. Moreover, they do not exploit available (though possibly mismatched) knowledge regarding the SS model, and operate in a black-box manner, lacking the interpretability of model-based tracking. An emerging paradigm for combining model-based signal processing with data-driven approaches is that of model-based deep learning (DL) [48], [49], [50], [51], [52]. Model-based DL encompasses a set of methodologies for fusing statistical models with DL to enhance interpretability, reduce the need for extensive labeled data, and provide reliable predictions [51]. In the context of tracking in dynamic systems, the KalmanNet algorithm proposed in [53] augments the EKF with a dedicated RNN, which enables learning to track in partially known SS models while preserving the SS-aware EKF. While its hybrid model-based/data-driven design was shown to improve accuracy under mismatched and nonlinear models [53], reduce latency [54], provide confidence measures [55], [56], and facilitate learning [57], its complexity significantly increases when processing high-dimensional signals. Existing extensions for mitigating complexity do so by mapping the observations into a low-dimensional subspace [58], thus not
handling high-dimensional state settings, as in tracking graph signals in large networks. This motivates accounting for the graph structure in model-based/data-driven tracking of graph signals. In this paper, we propose GSP-KalmanNet, a hybrid datadriven/model-based algorithm for real-time low-complexity tracking of graph signals. GSP-KalmanNet is based on the integration of GSP properties into the KalmanNet methodology by identifying the suitable graph filter for handling the complexity and nonlinearity of the SS model, while augmenting its operation with a dedicated RNN to cope with mismatches and unknown stochasticity. The resulting GSP-KalmanNet is suitable for tracking graph signals in a scalable and low-complexity manner, and learns to operate in possibly mismatched and approximated dynamic modeling. Specifically, we first assume that the dynamic system of graph signals can be described as a known nonlinear SS model, where the main challenge stems from its high dimensionality and nonlinearity. For such settings, we develop GSP-EKF, which alleviates the complexity of the EKF by tracking in the graph frequency domain, while taking the Kalman gain (KG) to be a graph filter. We analyze the GSP-EKF, showcasing the complexity reduction of its KG computation, and characterize analytical conditions for it to achieve the EKF performance. By identifying GSP-EKF as the suitable modelbased algorithm in the presence of full domain knowledge, we follow the KalmanNet methodology and augment it into GSPKalmanNet, addressing partial knowledge of the SS model. By identifying the graph filter KG of the GSP-EKF as its specific computation encapsulating the missing domain knowledge, we augment it with a dedicated RNN that preserves the scalable graph filter operation. We propose a learning algorithm that treats the overall tracking algorithm as a discriminative machine learning architecture [59], training the internal RNN of GSP-KalmanNet to produce a graph filter that minimizes the mean-squared error (MSE) in the GSP-EKF overall flow. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:
• Identifying the Core Challenges of Tracking Graph Signals: Our derivation is based on a principled formulation of the inherent challenges associated with tracking graph signals. These include the inherent complexity when processing high-dimensional graph signals, the likely presence of mismatches in the graph topology or SS modeling, and the expected unknown stochasticity.
• GFT-Based Processing as Key for Alleviating Complexity in Tracking: We rigorously pinpoint the usage of graph structure via processing in the GFT domain as a means for alleviating the complexity and scalability issues that arise when processing graph signals.
• GSP-Informed DNN-Augmented Algorithm: We leverage the above contributions to propose GSP-KalmanNet algorithm. Our novel algorithm is based on a principled combination of a set of key signal processing techniques. It combines GSP tools to alleviate complexity by exploiting spatial structure, with SS model-based methods to leverage temporal domain knowledge, integrated
