II. SYSTEM MODEL AND PRELIMINARIES
In this section, we introduce the considered model of a dynamic system of graph signals, which sets the ground for our derivations of the tracking algorithms in the following sections. In particular, we present the GSP notations and graph frequency domain filtering in Subsection II-A. Then, we describe the SS formulation and the considered tracking of a networked data problem in Subsection II-B. Finally, we review the necessary preliminaries for our derivation of GSP-KalmanNet, which are the model-based EKF and data-aided KalmanNet of [53], in Subsections II-C and II-D, respectively.
A. GSP Background
We consider an undirected, connected, weighted graph G(V, E, W) representing, e.g., a power grid or a sensor network topology. Here, V and E are sets of nodes (vertices) and edges, respectively, and W ∈ RN×N is the non-negative weighted ad
jacency matrix, where N = |V| is the number of nodes. If there is an edge (i, j) ∈ E, the entry [W]i,j represents the weight of the edge; otherwise, [W]i,j = 0. In general, graph signals are defined as follows [2]:
Definition II.1 (Graph signal): A graph signal z with entries in R is a function z : V → RN defined on the nodes of G. A common approach to designing filters for graph signals is based on the GFT. To formulate this, we use the Laplacian
matrix of the graph, which is defined as L = diag(W · 1) − W. The eigenvalue decomposition (EVD) of L is given by
L = VΛV . (1)
In (1), Λ is a diagonal matrix consisting of the eigenvalues of L, λ1 < λ2 ≤ . . . ≤ λN , the columns of V are comprised of the associated eigenvectors, and V = V−1. This EVD is used to define the GFT over G [3]: Definition II.2 (GFT): The GFT of a graph signal defined over a graph G with Laplacian L is
