Similarly, the inverse GFT is given by V ̃z. Linear and shiftinvariant graph filters of various graph shift operators play essential roles in GSP with various applications [60]. A common type of such a graph filter, which arises from the GFT in Definition II.2, is the frequency domain graph filter, defined as follows [61]: Definition II.3: A frequency domain graph filter defined over G with Laplacian L is a function g(·) applied to the Laplacian, which allows the following EVD:
g(L) = Vg(Λ)V , (3)
where g(Λ) is a diagonal matrix. In addition, the diagonal elements of g(Λ) that are associated with eigenvalues of L with a multiplicity greater than 1 are all equal, i.e., if λk+1 = λk, then g(λk+1) = g(λk).
One of the benefits of frequency domain graph filters stems from their reduced complexity when formulating their operation in the GFT domain. If y ̃ is the output of a frequency domain graph filter applied to a frequency domain graph signal x ̃, i.e., y ̃ = g(L)x ̃, then it holds that
[y ̃]n = g(λn)[x ̃]n, ∀n ∈ 1, . . . , N , (4)
where g(λn) is the graph frequency response of the filter at graph frequency λn. Consequently, instead of filtering with a complexity that grows quadratically with N (multiplying by an N × N matrix in the vertex domain), it has a complexity that only grows linearly with N via (4).
