We focus on tracking with partially known SS models. Specifically, we aim to cope with the following challenges: C.1 The size of the graph signal, N , which is the number of nodes, can be very large, causing high complexity and affecting real-time applicability. C.2 The graph topology, which can be captured by the Laplacian matrix L, may be misspecified, both in its connectivity pattern and the weights. C.3 The distributions of the noise signals, et and vt, are unknown and may be non-Gaussian. C.4 The state-evolution function, f (·), and the measurement function, h(·), are available, but may be mismatched. Challenge C.1 is commonly encountered in graph signals due to the growing size of typical virtual and physical networks. Challenge C.2 corresponds to the case where one is unaware of the true weights of some of the edges, possibly even wrongly assuming the presence or absence of some edges in a graph. Challenge C.3 stems from the fact that stochasticity in graphical data is often non-Gaussian, while the presence of mismatches in the model functions in Challenge C.4 is common in realworld tracking, where the physical phenomena cannot be fully described by simple mathematical equations. To cope with the various unknown characteristics of (5) encapsulated in Challenges C.2–C.4, we utilize a labeled dataset. The data-set is comprised of D trajectories, in which the dth trajectory is a sequence of paired graph signals of observations and states in length Td. The data-set is given by:
