Our proposed algorithm for tackling Challenges C.1-C.4 is detailed in Section IV. The design follows a model-based DL methodology [50], [51], [64], where DL tools are used to augment and empower existing model-based algorithms though replacing the parts that rely on missing domain knowledge with learnable components. Our method builds upon the properties of GSP, specifically in the graph frequency domain, which enables to reduce the classical EKF complexity when dealing with large signals (Challenge C.1). To describe the proposed algorithm, in the following two subsections, we review preliminaries of tracking in partially known SS models in a data-driven fashion, building upon the recently proposed KalmanNet of [53] (presented in Subsection II-D), which augments the classic EKF (presented in Subsection II-C).
C. Preliminaries: EKF
Tracking signals in SS models in regular domains (i.e., not over irregular graphs) is widely studied (see, e.g., [10, Ch. 10]). One of the most common algorithms, which is suitable for nonlinear models where the noise signals are Gaussian, the SS model is fully known, and the dimensions of the system are relatively small, i.e., in the absence of Challenges C.1-C.4, is the EKF [65]. The algorithm follows the operation of the KF, combining prediction based on the previous estimate with an update based on the current observation, while extending it to nonlinear SS models. In particular, the EKF first predicts the next state and observation based on xˆt−1 via
xˆt|t−1 = f (xˆt−1); yˆt|t−1 = h(xˆt|t−1). (8)
Then, the initial prediction is updated using a matrix Kt, known as the Kalman gain (KG), which dictates the balance between relying on the state evolution function f (·) through (8) and the current observation yt. In particular, the estimator is computed as
xˆt = Kt · Δyt + xˆt|t−1, (9)
where Δyt yt − yˆt|t−1. The KG is calculated via
Kt = Σˆ t|t−1 · ˆHt · Sˆ−1
