D. Preliminaries: KalmanNet
Challenges C.1-C.4 significantly limit the applicability of the EKF for the setup detailed in Subsection II-B. For instance, the distributions of the noise signals must be known in order to compute the matrices in (11). KalmanNet, proposed in [53], is a hybrid model-based/data-driven architecture, which leverages data as in (7) to tackle Challenges C.3-C.4 (but not Challenges C.1-C.2). KalmanNet builds on the insight that the missing and mismatched domain knowledge is encapsulated in the computation of the KG, Kt in (10). Consequently, it augments the EKF with a DL component by replacing the computation of the KG with a dedicated RNN-based architecture. The KG is computed using the features that are selected as ones that are indicative of the state and measurement noise statistics. For example, these features can be chosen as Δyt yt − yˆt|t−1 and Δxt xˆt − xˆt−1. Accordingly, KalmanNet sets the KG estimation to be:
Kt(θ) = gKG
θ (Δyt, Δxt), (14)
where gKG
θ (·) denotes the RNN mapping with parameters θ. The rest of the filtering operation is the same via (8), while replacing the state estimate in (9) with
xˆt = Kt(θ) · Δyt + xˆt|t−1. (15)
The weights of the RNN, θ, were learned from a data-set (7), based on the regularized MSE loss function (6). During training, the gradients of the loss with respect to θ propagate through the entire EKF pipeline to learn the computation of the KG as the one yielding the state estimate best matching the data in the sense of the MSE loss. By doing so, KalmanNet converts the EKF into a trainable discriminative model [59], where the data D is used to directly learn the KG, bypassing the need to enforce any model over the noise statistics and able to handle domain knowledge mismatches as described in Challenges C.3C.4. An illustration of the algorithm block diagram can be seen in Fig. 1. KalmanNet was shown to successfully cope with Challenges C.3-C.4 when applied to signals with relatively small dimensionality [53]. However, in the considered setup, where the cardinality of the graph signals (i.e., N ) can be very large, KalmanNet may become highly complex (as its number of
RNN neurons grows with N 3 [53]), significantly impacting the computational burden and data requirements of its training and inference. Since the state is a high-dimensional graph signal, one cannot mitigate complexity by mapping the observations into a low-dimensional subspace, as done in [58], [66]. This indicates that in order to jointly tackle Challenges C.1-C.4, one must account for the graph structure of the signals in the algorithm design, which is the basic principle in our derivation of GSP-KalmanNet in the following sections.
III. TRACKING IN THE GRAPH FREQUENCY DOMAIN
