GSP-EKF has the potential to improve the performance in terms of MSE, interpretability, robustness, complexity, flexibility, and stability compared to the conventional EKF. These advantages have previously been substantiated for static estimation that is based on GSP methodologies [18], [20].
2) Computational Complexity: The GSP-EKF is particularly designed to cope with the excessive complexity of applying the EKF to high-dimensional graph signals (Challenge C.1). We thus commence our analysis of the GSP-EKF with an analysis of the computational savings, as compared with the standard EKF. Unlike the standard EKF, the GSP-EKF does not require non-diagonal matrix inversion at each time step for computing the KG, as in (10). In fact, the KG of the GSP-EKF can be calculated in a vectorized form with a complexity of O(N ) multiplications instead of O(N 3) (due to inverting an N × N matrix) for the conventional EKF. It is noted that the transformation of the system to the graph frequency domain requires the computation of the EVD of the Laplacian matrix of order O(N 3) (Step 1 of Algorithm 1). However, this calculation can be carried out offline, as it only has to be done once for a given graph, and possibly with low-complexity methods [69]. Thus, its complexity is considered separately from that of the online processing of each incoming sample, which is the core source of processing latency and thus the focus of our complexity analysis. Finally, it should be noted that the transformation of each signal to the graph frequency domain requires multiplication by N × N matrix, V (O(N 2)), as opposed to O(N 3) in the unconstrained EKF.
3) Orthogonal Graph Frequencies: While the GSP-EKF is in general different from the EKF, in some cases, the filters coincide. One such setting of orthogonal graph frequencies is characterized by the following theorem.
