IV. GSP-KALMANNET
The GSP-EKF derived above only partially copes with the challenges discussed in Subsection II-B. Specifically, it only tackles Challenge C.1 (Complexity). However, its suitability for tracking graph signals indicates that in order to jointly tackle Challenges C.1-C.4, we should leverage data (7) and convert the GSP-EKF into a hybrid discriminative model-based/datadriven architecture [59], in the same spirit of converting the EKF to the KalmanNet in [53]). The resulting GSP-KalmanNet algorithm presented in this section is a hybrid, interpretable, and data-efficient scheme for tracking graph signals in partially known nonlinear dynamics. To introduce GSP-KalmanNet, we begin by explaining its high level operation in Subsection IV-A, and detail the features processed by its RNN and its specific architecture in Subsection IV-B. Then, we present how GSPKalmanNet is trained in Subsection IV-C, and provide a discussion in Subsection IV-D.
A. High Level Architecture
While the design of GSP-KalmanNet follows the methodology used in formulating KalmanNet (see Subsection II-D), its architecture differs from that proposed in [53]. This stems from the fact that the complexity of KalmanNet, which is based on the conventional EKF, scales cubically with the dimensions of the signals (O(N 3)), and thus, cannot be used to track graph signals in large networks. Previous adaptations of KalmanNet for high-dimensional signals, e.g., [58], were tailored for low-dimensional states and only high-dimensional observations. Accordingly, we derive here the GSP-KalmanNet from the GSP-EKF detailed in Subsection III-B, leveraging its
