D. Discussion
The proposed GSP-KalmanNet is particularly tailored for tracking graph signals under the common realistic difficulties highlighted in Challenges C.1-C.4. It builds on the understanding that in order to simultaneously cope with incomplete domain knowledge and the inherent complexity associated with high-dimensional graph signals, one cannot rely on conventional EKF and its augmentations, but rather develop a dedicated machine learning architecture that arises from a graph signal-oriented tracking algorithm. For this reason, our first step in deriving GSP-KalmanNet is the development of GSP-EKF in Section III, followed by its conversion into a trainable algorithm that simultaneously benefits from the available (though possibly approximated) knowledge of the SS model alongside learning from data. The augmentation of the KG Kt computation by a DNN Kt(θ) is not only a parametric graph filter as proposed in,
