V. EMPIRICAL STUDY
In this section, we experimentally evaluate GSP-KalmanNet1 and compare its performance with those of the filtering modelbased/data-driven benchmarks, detailed in the experimental setup in Subsection V-A. We consider cases with both full and partial domain knowledge. In the first study, presented in Subsection V-B, we consider two synthetic data-sets using nonlinear Gaussian SS models. In the second study in Subsection V-C we consider the practical application domain of power system monitoring [5], [6], [72] in case of both Gaussian and nonGaussian noise. In Subsection V-D we numerically demonstrate some of the operational gains which arise from the interpretable design of GSP-KalmanNet. We conclude with complexity and latency evaluation in Subsection V-E.
A. Experimental Setup
We simulate dynamic systems that showcase the online tracking capabilities using both synthetic examples and real-world power grid trajectories. In our experiments, we consider both observations and hidden states over a known (though possibly inaccurate) graph structure with N nodes and adjacency matrix (A for an unweighted graph in Subsection V-B and W for a weighted graph in Subsection V-C). The observations are corrupted by two types of measurement noises: 1) An i.i.d. Gaussian noise, vt, with covariance matrix R = r2 · I; and 2) An exponential noise with different levels of parameter λ, used in the setting detailed in Subsection V-C. The evolution process noise is driven by i.i.d. Gaussian noise et with covariance matrix Q = q2 · I, setting a fixed ratio of log(q2/r2) = −20 dB in the Gaussian case, and log(q2/λ) = −20 dB in the exponential case. In each experiment, we generate D = 2, 000 trajectories
